/**
 * @page risk_analysis_k T2.3 — Risk Analysis = K (K > 1)
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section ra_problem Problem Statement
 *
 * Given a valid reviewer assignment, determine whether the assignment remains
 * feasible if <b>any K reviewers simultaneously fail</b> to complete their reviews.
 * A reviewer "failing" means all their assigned submissions lose those reviews entirely.
 *
 * @section ra_approach Approach
 *
 * The naive approach is to enumerate all possible subsets of K reviewers,
 * remove them and rerun Max-Flow for each subset. However this has complexity
 * O(C(M,K) × MaxFlow) which grows exponentially with K.
 *
 * A more efficient approach combines Max-Flow with a <b>minimum vertex cut</b> formulation.
 *
 * @subsection ra_step1 Step 1 — Node Splitting
 *
 * Transform each reviewer node r into two nodes r_in and r_out connected
 * by an internal edge with capacity 1:
 *
 * @code
 * Source → Submission → r_in ──(1)──► r_out → Sink
 * @endcode
 *
 * This encodes "this reviewer can be removed" as cutting the internal edge.
 *
 * @subsection ra_step2 Step 2 — Find Minimum K-Cut
 *
 * The problem reduces to finding the <b>minimum vertex cut</b> of size K
 * in the reviewer layer. If the minimum vertex cut has size < K, then there
 * exist K reviewers whose removal breaks the assignment.
 *
 * This can be solved using K successive max-flow computations on the node-split graph:
 *
 * @code
 * for i = 1 to K:
 *     find min-cut in current graph
 *     remove the reviewer corresponding to the cut edge
 *     update graph
 *     if assignment no longer feasible:
 *         report this set of reviewers as risky
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection ra_step3 Step 3 — Enumerate Risky Sets
 *
 * For reporting, collect all sets of K reviewers whose removal breaks feasibility:
 *
 * @code
 * RiskySet = {}
 * for each combination C of K reviewers:
 *     build graph without reviewers in C
 *     run MaxFlow
 *     if totalFlow < expectedFlow:
 *         RiskySet.add(C)
 * report RiskySet sorted
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section ra_complexity Complexity Analysis
 *
 * | Approach                  | Time Complexity          | Space    |
 * |---------------------------|--------------------------|----------|
 * | Naive enumeration         | O(C(M,K) × V × E²)      | O(V+E)   |
 * | Min vertex cut (K passes) | O(K × V × E²)           | O(V+E)   |
 *
 * Where:
 * - M = number of reviewers
 * - V = N + M + 2 (total vertices)
 * - E = O(N×M) (total edges)
 * - C(M,K) = M! / (K! × (M-K)!) grows exponentially
 *
 * The min vertex cut approach is <b>polynomial in K</b> making it practical
 * for small K, while the naive approach becomes infeasible even for moderate
 * values like K=5 with 20 reviewers (C(20,5) = 15504 iterations).
 *
 * @section ra_example Non-Trivial Example
 *
 * @subsection ra_input Input
 * @code
 * #Submissions
 * 1, "Paper A", Author A, a@up.pt, 1,
 * 2, "Paper B", Author B, b@up.pt, 1,
 * 3, "Paper C", Author C, c@up.pt, 1,
 * #
 * #Reviewers
 * 1, Reviewer One,   r1@up.pt, 1,
 * 2, Reviewer Two,   r2@up.pt, 1,
 * 3, Reviewer Three, r3@up.pt, 1,
 * #Parameters
 * MinReviewsPerSubmission, 2
 * MaxReviewsPerReviewer, 3
 * PrimaryReviewerExpertise, 1
 * SecondaryReviewerExpertise, 0
 * PrimarySubmissionDomain, 1
 * SecondarySubmissionDomain, 0
 * #Control
 * GenerateAssignments, 1
 * RiskAnalysis, 2
 * OutputFileName, "output.csv"
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection ra_reasoning Why This Is Non-Trivial for K=2
 *
 * - 3 submissions × 2 reviews needed = 6 total reviews required
 * - 3 reviewers × 3 max reviews = 9 total capacity available
 * - Remove any 1 reviewer: 2 remaining × 3 max = 6 capacity → still feasible ✓
 * - Remove any 2 reviewers: 1 remaining × 3 max = 3 capacity < 6 needed → infeasible ✗
 *
 * @subsection ra_output Expected Output
 * @code
 * #Risk Analysis: 2
 * 1, 2
 * 1, 3
 * 2, 3
 * @endcode
 *
 * All pairs are risky because with only 3 reviewers and MinReviewsPerSubmission=2,
 * removing any 2 reviewers breaks feasibility.
 */

/**
 * @page general_formulation T2.4 — General Formulation (Mode 3, All Domains)
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section gf_problem Problem Statement
 *
 * Find an assignment of submissions to reviewers considering
 * <b>all domain combinations</b> (primary/secondary for both submissions
 * and reviewers) that:
 * - Gives every submission at least MinReviewsPerSubmission reviews
 * - Assigns each reviewer at most MaxReviewsPerReviewer submissions
 * - <b>Maximises primary domain matches</b> over secondary domain matches
 *
 * @section gf_why Why Simple Max-Flow Is Insufficient
 *
 * A single Max-Flow pass on mode 3 finds <b>a</b> valid assignment but not
 * the <b>optimal</b> one. Consider:
 *
 * @code
 * Sub1 (primary=1)    Rev1 (primary=1, secondary=2)
 * Sub2 (primary=2)    Rev2 (primary=3, secondary=1)
 * @endcode
 *
 * Single pass might produce:
 * @code
 * Sub1 → Rev2 (via secondary match, domain 1)
 * Sub2 → Rev1 (via secondary match, domain 2)
 * @endcode
 *
 * But the optimal assignment is:
 * @code
 * Sub1 → Rev1 (via PRIMARY match, domain 1)  ← preferred
 * Sub2 → Rev1 (via secondary match, domain 2)
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section gf_approach1 Approach 1 — Min-Cost Max-Flow
 *
 * Model the problem as a <b>minimum cost maximum flow</b> problem by
 * assigning costs to edges:
 *
 * @code
 * Source → Submission:          cost = 0,  capacity = MinReviewsPerSubmission
 * Sub → Rev (primary match):    cost = 0,  capacity = 1   ← preferred
 * Sub → Rev (secondary match):  cost = 1,  capacity = 1   ← penalised
 * Reviewer → Sink:              cost = 0,  capacity = MaxReviewsPerReviewer
 * @endcode
 *
 * Running <b>MCMF (Min-Cost Max-Flow)</b> finds the maximum flow with minimum
 * total cost, which naturally prioritises primary matches (cost=0) over
 * secondary matches (cost=1).
 *
 * The algorithm uses Successive Shortest Paths with SPFA to find the cheapest
 * augmenting path:
 *
 * @code
 * while (augmenting path exists from Source to Sink):
 *     find shortest (cheapest) path using SPFA
 *     augment flow along that path
 *     update residual graph
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section gf_approach2 Approach 2 — Two-Pass Max-Flow (Practical Approximation)
 *
 * A simpler approach that achieves near-optimal results in practice:
 *
 * <b>Pass 1</b> — Build graph with only primary domain edges, run Max-Flow:
 * @code
 * Only add Sub→Rev edge if primary(Sub) == primary(Rev)
 * Run Edmonds-Karp → saturates all primary matches first
 * @endcode
 *
 * <b>Pass 2</b> — Add secondary edges for under-reviewed submissions, run Max-Flow again:
 * @code
 * For each submission with flow < MinReviewsPerSubmission:
 *     add Sub→Rev edges for secondary domain matches
 * Run Edmonds-Karp again → fills remaining gaps with secondary matches
 * @endcode
 *
 * This guarantees primary matches are always preferred while remaining polynomial:
 * @code
 * O(2 × V × E²) = O(V × E²)
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section gf_complexity Complexity Comparison
 *
 * | Approach              | Time                   | Optimality                          |
 * |-----------------------|------------------------|-------------------------------------|
 * | Single pass Max-Flow  | O(V × E²)              | No — secondary may displace primary |
 * | Two-pass Max-Flow     | O(V × E²)              | Near-optimal — primary preferred    |
 * | Min-Cost Max-Flow     | O(V × E² × log V)      | Optimal — guaranteed primary priority|
 * | Brute force           | O(C(M,N) × N!)         | Exact but exponential               |
 *
 * Where E = O(N×M) in the worst case for mode 3, since every reviewer can
 * potentially match every submission through some combination of domains.
 *
 * @section gf_hardness Why Mode 3 Is Fundamentally Harder
 *
 * In mode 1 the graph is <b>sparse</b> — each reviewer connects only to
 * submissions in their exact primary domain. In mode 3 the graph becomes
 * <b>dense</b> — a reviewer with primary=1 and secondary=2 can connect to
 * all submissions with primary=1, secondary=1, primary=2, or secondary=2.
 *
 * In the worst case this yields O(N×M) edges compared to O(N+M) for mode 1,
 * making E² grow from O((N+M)²) to O(N²M²) — a quadratic blowup in the
 * exponent of the complexity.
 *
 * Furthermore the <b>optimal assignment problem</b> in mode 3 (maximising
 * primary matches subject to flow constraints) is equivalent to a
 * <b>weighted bipartite matching</b> problem, which in its general form
 * requires the Hungarian algorithm or MCMF — both significantly more
 * expensive than plain Max-Flow.
 */
